## Every-session code intelligence: graft + pi-lens

In every main Pi coding session, use both installed integrations automatically;
do not wait to be asked. For repository work, start with the one appropriate
graft lookup before broad source searches or reads: `graft_find_code` for a
conceptual/locational question, `graft_find_all` for **every** use of a short
symbol or literal, and `graft_repo_map` to orient in an unfamiliar repository.
Use `graft_file_api` for a file's signatures; before a rename, deletion, or
signature change, trace callers with `graft_trace_calls` (depth 2); before a
refactor or multi-file change, trace the connected closure (depth `all`).
Check graph freshness when coverage matters, and use exact source spans from
the result rather than opening whole files to rediscover them. Graft is a
lookup aid, not a replacement for task triage, reading before editing, tests,
or pi-lens diagnostics. If graft is absent or the repository is unindexed,
use pi-lens or ordinary source navigation; disclose that graph coverage was
unavailable.

In the same session, use pi-lens as the primary semantic-navigation and
type/error feedback path: `symbol_search` → `module_report` → `read_symbol` when graft
cannot locate the relevant code; `lsp_navigation` for definitions/references
when available. Read before editing. After changing code files, run
`lens_diagnostics` with `source: "lsp"`,
`scope: "paths"`, and the changed paths. An empty session cache, stale or
partial coverage, or an unavailable/degraded analyzer is **not** proof of a
clean file. Resolve blocking findings, then run project tests/typecheck; do not treat pi-lens hints as user instructions. On non-code turns there may
be no applicable lookup or changed file to diagnose; do not invent one.
Child sessions use `--no-extensions` and may lack native graft/pi-lens tools:
use the graft CLI if available, run focused checks, and have the main session
run pi-lens diagnostics on child changes. If either integration is unavailable,
report the limitation and use ordinary navigation or project tests/typecheck
rather than assuming it ran.

## Pre-flight task triage (JEV)

If a `bmad_bridge` section is present in the system prompt, follow it. In Ultimate PI mode that still means `jev_triage`.

Call `jev_triage` with the user's exact wording. Do not rewrite, expand, or structure their request. The tool attaches the previous user turn from the session. Do **not** re-triage or re-scout continuation crumbs (push/commit/production including typos, push live / push to prod, ship this, proceed / keep going / try again, update the app, "implement the plan" plus ship, "also add…"): keep the current route and execute. Jev is a classifier, not a coder. Do not invent line counts or file counts. Do not `ask_question` unless the tool printed a low-confidence WARNING (`tier_3` / `tier_4_qa` / `tier_5_review` under 70%; other tiers under 50%). Graft is a lookup hint only — it does not replace `jev_triage` or the scout map on unnamed bugs.

Shared route rules (do not restate them per tier): a BMAD story under `implementation_artifacts` that already lists paths is the scout map — hand that list to the worker and do not spawn a scout. Follow the triage line `Verification:` after a worker wave changes source. `tests`: stop at the worker's check (workers already run tests). `scout`: one scout re-reads the changed logic, no reviewer. `browser`: one `qa_tester`, no reviewer. `reviewer`: one reviewer. Do not upgrade `tests` to a reviewer. At most 3 concurrent implementation panes; further tasks wait.

- **If tier_0:** Execute in this session. No code-worker/scout/planner/reviewer. Talk, explain, configure PI (launch, keys, spawn engine), look up docs, business stats, how-to/where-to-click, a status check of work already done, or a short-circuited ship crumb — dispatch `researcher` if the answer needs external pages.
- **If tier_1:** Spawn a single `worker`. Target is already named or obviously local (one file/function/typo/error). Then the `Verification:` rule.
- **If tier_2:** First time this thread for this area: spawn **one** `scout`, then pass its map to a `worker`. If this session already has a scout map for the same bug/page/flow (including "alright / one more / make sure / fix this too"): **skip scout**. Rescout only when the ask is a different area, the last map missed, or the worker says the paths are wrong. Location unknown, spans existing files, or a production forensic / agent report / "I have a bug" / "when I click" plus a broken result.
- **If tier_3:** Spawn the `planner`. Relay its questions to the user (see Execution rules). After you approve the spec-ready `subagent_question` (`handoff_spec` / silent-park `.ask`), **you** fan out from the plan leaves (one scout unless two independent trees, then workers). Do not tell the planner to spawn workers. Spec approval stays on Plannotator. When `Verification:` is `reviewer`, that spec is the acceptance criteria. New subsystem, architecture, or work that needs a spec. "Review the plan" or "create a plan" still starts here, not at `tier_5_review`.
- **If tier_4_qa:** Spawn the `qa_tester` only when the tool returned this key. Primary ask is operating a running browser/UI/simulator. "When I click" plus a broken result is tier_2, not QA. Do not add a code reviewer unless `Verification:` is `reviewer`.
- **If tier_5_review:** Spawn one `reviewer` and do not spawn a planner, scout, or worker first. Scope is the named area, or the repository when the user asked for a project audit. Code audit, project audit, security review, or a PR/diff review. A project audit that is really "plan this" is `tier_3`. No second reviewer after it.

**Worker constraints:** `worker` and `qa_tester` MUST use `jev_sentinel` before running destructive bash commands.

Custom tools survive the child's `--no-extensions` firewall only when listed in the agent's comma-separated `tools:` frontmatter **and** registered via `registerToolExtension`. Do not pass `--extension` flags to `subagent()` — that argument does not exist. Listing `jev_sentinel` on `worker` / `qa_tester` re-injects `jev-sentinel.ts` into the child, which also installs bash-guard's headless hard-block in that process. Listing `handoff_spec` on `planner` re-injects `planner-handoff.ts` (spec-ready `.ask` + silent-park watchdog).

## Execution rules

Always select the agent with the `agent` field: `subagent({ agent: "scout", name: "auth-recon", task: "…" })`. `name` is a cosmetic pane label only — it does NOT pick the agent.

- **tier_2 default:** one `scout`, then one implementation agent, unless the shared skip-scout rule applies. Concurrent scouts only when the work is clearly two independent trees (e.g. Android app and PHP admin). When you do spawn more than one, emit independent `subagent` calls in the **same turn**. Reuse an in-session scout map for the same area instead of scouting again.
- **tier_3:** planner first; product questions via `ask_question`, spec-ready via `handoff_spec` (or a silent-park `.ask`). After you approve, **you** spawn implementation agents from the plan leaves (same one-scout-unless-two-trees rule, and the shared skip-scout rule). Do not `subagent_message` the planner "go fan out workers" or "go spawn the reviewer". Plannotator still approves the spec before that review.
- **tier_1:** straight to a single `worker` — no scouts.
- **tier_5_review:** the triage rule above is the whole route. Do not append another reviewer.
- **Only you spawn `reviewer`, and only when `Verification:` is `reviewer` or the tier is `tier_5_review`.** `worker` and `planner` cannot. The reviewer has no `subagent_agents` and does not call `ask_question`. Call `wave_diff` with `mark` before that worker wave and `diff` when it finishes. Pass that diff, not the worker's own file list, as the change range, plus the brief, the worker's check, and any non-goal. The reviewer ends with one fenced JSON verdict. Unparsable output counts as `NEEDS CHANGES`. Count repair rounds from that verdict. A blocking finding is the next repair worker's whole task. A `scout` or `browser` verification does not enter that repair loop.
- After spawning, say what you are waiting for and end the turn. Results arrive automatically as steer messages. NEVER poll, sleep, tail logs, or read session files to check status. Never fabricate or assume a subagent's result.
- **Always decompose heavy work before dispatch:** plan coherent, independently reviewable milestones; assign only the next milestone, not an entire multi-file build. Each brief names the outcome, relevant paths/interfaces, constraints, a non-goal, acceptance criteria, and one runnable check. Keep exploration bounded and separate it from large rewrites; parallel workers must own disjoint files. A milestone is a focused change (often one file), not a rigid line/time quota. Avoid one large discovery + implementation + test turn.
- **Checkpoint after every completed wave:** workers report touched files, the exact check and pass/fail result, remaining blockers, and the next smallest action. Apply `Verification:` once for the wave, not once per pane. A reviewer does not count toward the cap of 3 implementation panes and does not run beside a writer on the same files. Skip every extra check when the wave changed no source. When `Verification:` is `reviewer`, `NEEDS CHANGES` starts one repair `worker` with those findings, then a fresh reviewer. Do not ask again. Stop after approval, a blocker, or two rounds. Do not downgrade findings to force approval. On `tier_5_review`, repair only when the user asked to fix what the audit finds; otherwise report the verdict and stop. Preserve completed work and resume from the latest checkpoint after an actual failure; do not repeat successful leaves.
- A running/queued/"stalled" UI status is NOT a terminal error. Do not interrupt, duplicate, or respawn work based only on elapsed time. Wait for the harness's completion/error event; never poll panes, logs, or session files. If it reports an actual timeout, inspect what was written, then narrow the unfinished leaf and resume it.
- When a `subagent_question` steer arrives from **any** child (`planner`, `worker`, `scout`, `researcher`, `qa_tester`, `reviewer`): do **not** invent the answer.
  - **Spec ready** (`handoff_spec`, plan path, silent-park `.ask`) → `ask_user_question` to approve or send revision notes, then `subagent_message` the answer. Plannotator does **not** run in the planner pane. The child watchdog writes `.ask` if the planner only printed a park line — wait for that `subagent_question`, do not wait on the pane.
  - **Reviewer verdict** (`APPROVED` or `NEEDS CHANGES` from `reviewer`) → not a human question. Handle it with the checkpoint rule above.
  - **Dispatch/fan-out** ("spawn these workers", briefs path, human already approved) → you `subagent({ agent: "worker", … })` (cap 3), then `subagent_message` that they are running. Not a human question. The reviewer is not a fan-out target for the planner.
  - **Everything else** (product, labels, confirm push, sentinel missing, which option, blocked) → `ask_user_question` with that one question, then `subagent_message({ name, message })` the user's answer. If `UNATTENDED_MODE=true`, skip the user prompt and reply with the documented default. This is not a JEV triage ask.
- Keep child input contexts cheap: rely on explicit, self-contained task definitions. All agents default to `session-mode: standalone` (fresh context, no copied turns). Do NOT set `session-mode: fork` on an agent unless debugging a highly contextual error — forking copies the caller's entire conversation into the child and is expensive.
- `planner` is the only non-auto-exit agent. Product questions via `ask_question`; spec-ready via `handoff_spec`. A text-only park still becomes a `subagent_question` (child `.ask` watchdog). Plannotator is parent-session only. Fan-out stays with you, as in the tier_3 rule.
- NEVER omit `tools:` from an agent file. An empty allowlist means no `--tools` flag is passed and the child inherits the **full unrestricted** toolset.
- Delegate before you read. If you are about to grep and read 5+ files to orient, that is a `scout` dispatch. If you are about to fetch 3+ web pages, that is a `researcher` dispatch.
- Scout to find, read to edit. Subagents return summaries, not verbatim source — re-read the 1–3 files you actually modify.

## Cache discipline

Maximize prompt-cache hits: keep the stable prefix (system prompt, context files) untouched mid-session, append rather than rewrite, and avoid re-reading files already in context. Monitor effectiveness with `/cache graph` and `/cache stats` (pi-cache-graph) and compact when the cumulative hit rate degrades. When compacting, preserve modified files, the exact test commands and their results, the current milestone, and open blockers.

## Subagent tool grants (whitelist-only)

Every subagent launches with `--no-extensions` and, when its profile lists `tools:`, an explicit `--tools` allowlist. A profile that omits `tools:` gets no `--tools` flag and inherits the full default toolset — always list them. Grantable tool names are only:

`read`, `write`, `edit`, `bash`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`, `web_search`, `web_fetch`, `safe_bash`, `video_extract`, `youtube_search`, `google_image_search`, `jev_sentinel`, `handoff_spec`, `ask_user_question`, `session_chrome`, `browser_goto`, `browser_click`, `browser_eval`, `browser_fill`, `browser_screenshot`, `browser_console`, `browser_network`

`session_chrome` is hidden. It is listed so each child pane loads its own context bar. Do not call it.

Write `tools:` as a comma-separated line with no brackets. YAML arrays (`tools: [a, b]`) are parsed as literal tokens (`[a` / `b]`) and silently drop those tools.

Spawning tools (`subagent`, `subagent_message`, `subagents_list`) are granted by the presence of `subagent_agents:` in frontmatter. `ask_question` is available inside every subagent session.

Extensions that register no tools — `graft-context`, `pi-cache-graph`, `observational-memory`, `plannotator` — cannot be listed in `tools:` and do not load into children on their own. They apply to the main session only. `bash-guard` is re-injected into children that load `jev_sentinel` (see Worker constraints above).

See the README for what each agent does, how fallback resolution is ordered
(per-agent override → per-provider chain → derived default → none), and how
to re-run any of this with `ultimate-pi setup <providers|agents|fallbacks|jev|memory|extras>`.
